{
 "id": "present-active",
 "title": "Present Active Indicative",
 "course": "Verbs · Active Indicative",
 "subtitle": "Flashcards for all four conjugations plus 3rd -iō",
 "intro": "The present is the <b>present stem</b> (the infinitive minus -re: amā-, vidē-, mitte-, cape-, audī-) + the personal endings <span class=\"end\">-ō, -s, -t, -mus, -tis, -nt</span>.<ul><li><b>1st (amō):</b> the ā disappears in the 1st singular (am<b>ō</b>) and shortens before -t and -nt (ama<b>t</b>, ama<b>nt</b>).</li><li><b>2nd (videō):</b> the ē stays in every form (vide<b>ō</b>, vidē<b>s</b>), short before -t and -nt.</li><li><b>3rd (mittō):</b> the short e becomes <b>i</b> (mitti<b>s</b>, mitti<b>t</b>, mitti<b>mus</b>) and <b>u</b> in the 3rd plural (mittu<b>nt</b>).</li><li><b>3rd -iō (capiō) and 4th (audiō):</b> the i stays throughout, and the 3rd plural adds u: capi<b>unt</b>, audi<b>unt</b>. The 4th has a long ī (audīs, audīmus); the 3rd -iō has a short i (capis, capimus).</li></ul><p style=\"margin:8px 0 0\"><b>Translation:</b> the present has two English versions: “I love” or “I am loving.”</p>",
 "paradigmNote": "Endings: <span class=\"end\">-ō</span>, <span class=\"end\">-s</span>, <span class=\"end\">-t</span>, <span class=\"end\">-mus</span>, <span class=\"end\">-tis</span>, <span class=\"end\">-nt</span>",
